Home page

The home page is the main tool directory.

The main content should follow this hierarchy:

1. Elowen title

Near the top of the page, prominently display:

Elowen

Keep the presentation clean and spacious.

A short optional subtitle may be added if it improves the design, but do not add marketing-heavy copy.

For example, the general concept can be something similar to:

Simple tools for focused work.

Do not use this exact wording if something better fits the design.

2. Tool search

Below the title, include a search bar that allows users to search the available Elowen tools.

The search must work client-side and filter tool cards immediately.

Requirements:

* Search by tool name
* Search should be case-insensitive
* Clear empty state when no tools match
* Accessible label
* Search icon can be an SVG
* Include a clear button when text has been entered if appropriate
* Pressing Escape may clear the search where sensible

The search system should be built so more tools can easily be added later.

3. Tool grid

Below the search area, display all available tools as cards.

On sufficiently wide screens, display up to 4 cards per row.

Each card should contain:

* Tool name
* Brief description
* Appropriate SVG icon
* Clear clickable area

Cards should have subtle hover, focus, and press states.

Avoid exaggerated shadows or animations.

The current tool list contains only:

Elowen Calculator

Description should explain that it is a full-featured calculator for everyday and advanced calculations.

Clicking the card opens the calculator.

Design the home page architecture so additional tools can later be added easily without redesigning the whole page.

⸻

Elowen Calculator

Build a complete, polished calculator.

It should feel like a real productivity tool rather than a simple four-function demo.

The calculator must work entirely in the browser.

Basic operations

Support:

* Addition
* Subtraction
* Multiplication
* Division
* Decimal values
* Positive and negative numbers
* Percentages
* Parentheses
* Clear
* Clear entry where appropriate
* Backspace/delete
* Equals
* Proper mathematical order of operations

Scientific functionality

Include useful scientific functions such as:

* Square
* Square root
* Powers
* Arbitrary exponentiation
* Reciprocal
* Pi
* Euler’s number
* Absolute value
* Factorial where valid
* Natural logarithm
* Base-10 logarithm
* Exponential functions
* Sine
* Cosine
* Tangent
* Inverse trigonometric functions where practical

Include a switch between:

* Degrees
* Radians

If useful, scientific controls may be shown through an expandable scientific panel on smaller devices so the mobile interface stays usable.

Memory functions

Include calculator memory functionality:

* MC
* MR
* M+
* M-
* MS

Clearly indicate when calculator memory contains a value.

History

Include calculation history.

Each history item should show:

* Expression
* Result

Users should be able to:

* Review previous calculations
* Reuse a previous result
* Clear calculator history

History should persist locally between browser sessions unless the user clears it.

Keyboard support

Desktop users should be able to use a physical keyboard.

Support sensible mappings for:

* Numbers
* Decimal point
* +
* -
* *
* /
* Parentheses
* Enter
* Equals
* Backspace
* Delete/Escape where appropriate

Do not interfere with normal browser shortcuts.

Calculation safety and correctness

Do not implement calculation by blindly passing user-controlled strings into JavaScript eval().

Use a safer parsing/calculation approach.

Handle invalid calculations gracefully.

Examples:

* Division by zero
* Invalid factorial input
* Invalid square roots where unsupported
* Incomplete expressions
* Overflow
* Invalid function usage

Display understandable error states instead of crashing.

The calculator must recover easily after an error.

Calculator display

Create a clear display area showing:

* Current expression
* Current/result value

Large values should remain readable.

Support long expressions without destroying the page layout.

Use appropriate formatting for large or very small results.

Avoid unnecessary floating-point artifacts such as displaying:

0.30000000000000004

for a normal 0.1 + 0.2 calculation.

