Accessibility

Accessibility is required.

Implement:

* Semantic HTML
* Keyboard navigation
* Proper <button> elements for actions
* Form labels
* Meaningful accessible names
* Visible keyboard focus states
* Adequate color contrast
* Appropriate ARIA attributes only where actually necessary
* Respect for prefers-reduced-motion
* Touch-friendly control sizes

Do not rely only on color to communicate important states.

Icons used without accompanying text must have appropriate accessible labels.

Decorative SVGs should be hidden from screen readers where appropriate.

⸻

Animations and interaction design

Animations should be subtle and fast.

Good examples:

* Slight button press feedback
* Soft hover transitions
* Small card movement or surface changes
* Smooth opening/closing of panels

Avoid:

* Large bouncing effects
* Excessive scaling
* Flashy gradients
* Long transition delays
* Constant looping animations

Press states should use the Very Light Beige palette color.

Respect the user’s reduced-motion preference.

⸻

Technical requirements

Use standard web technologies that work directly on GitHub Pages:

* HTML
* CSS
* JavaScript

A framework is not required.

Prefer a simple implementation with minimal dependencies.

If using a dependency, only use it when it provides a clear benefit.

The website should not require:

* Node.js on the deployed server
* A backend
* PHP
* A database
* Authentication
* API keys

GitHub Pages should be able to serve the finished files directly.

Keep JavaScript modular and organized.

Keep CSS organized using reusable variables and components.

Avoid giant unstructured files when reasonable.

⸻

Progressive Web App and offline behavior

If it can be implemented cleanly without compromising GitHub Pages compatibility, make Elowen a lightweight Progressive Web App.

Possible features:

* Web app manifest
* App icons based on the existing Elowen image where appropriate
* Installable experience
* Basic offline support for Elowen’s own static pages and calculator

If implementing a service worker:

* Do not make updates difficult
* Avoid aggressive caching that causes users to remain stuck on old versions
* Use a sensible cache versioning strategy
* Do not cache unrelated third-party resources unnecessarily

The website should remain fully functional as a normal website even if PWA installation is never used.

